WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.594
Week three.

00:00:01.644 --> 00:00:03.198
Why do brands matter?

00:00:03.248 --> 00:00:10.042
Justin Papps from Tenet defines a brand as beliefs and perceptions that create a positive predisposition at the point of choice.

00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:12.723
So a brand lives in the customer's head,

00:00:12.773 --> 00:00:13.997
not your guidelines,

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:16.025
and the work happens before purchase.

00:00:16.075 --> 00:00:18.307
Marketing is persuasion.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.754
Brand first meant to burn.

00:00:20.804 --> 00:00:21.604
Then the mark showed ownership,

00:00:21.075 --> 00:00:22.464
then who made it,

00:00:22.514 --> 00:00:25.247
then a reputation for quality.

00:00:25.297 --> 00:00:27.822
Now it means what it says about you.

00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:33.294
Associations cluster around the founder, what the company does, and adjectives.

00:00:33.344 --> 00:00:35.948
Tesla shows the founder link cuts both ways.

00:00:35.998 --> 00:00:38.723
Keep the link positive and consistent.

00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:41.211
Brand exposures went from ten to twenty a day in the seventeen hundreds to ten thousand today,

00:00:41.261 --> 00:00:42.061
and our brains haven't grown.

00:00:41.756 --> 00:00:48.687
The battle moved from the aisle to a phone screen.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.119
Five reasons brands matter:

00:00:51.169 --> 00:00:52.180
economic engines,

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:53.477
behavioural shortcuts,

00:00:53.527 --> 00:00:54.786
strategy in action,

00:00:54.836 --> 00:00:55.789
shaping culture,

00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:57.670
and volatility moats.

00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.510
One, economic engines.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.781
A Louis Vuitton backpack costs fifteen point six times a Maverick and Co bag that beats it on compartments and warranty.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.396
An iPhone costs about four hundred and fifty to make and sells for up to sixteen hundred,

00:01:08.446 --> 00:01:14.186
a margin of sixty-seven percent plus.

00:01:14.236 --> 00:01:18.425
If brands didn't matter, Amazon would list products alphabetically.

00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:21.300
Two, behavioural shortcuts.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:22.150
Kahneman's System One is about ninety-five percent of thinking: fast and unconscious.

00:01:21.786 --> 00:01:29.661
System Two is slow and logical, for big decisions.

00:01:29.711 --> 00:01:31.987
Brands aim at System One.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:38.747
Brand strategy rests on four pillars: relevance, distinctiveness, consistency and availability.

00:01:38.797 --> 00:01:42.098
Land all four and you get cut-through and salience.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.297
Distinctive assets compound with age.

00:01:45.347 --> 00:01:47.054
But Ipsos found a paradox:

00:01:47.104 --> 00:01:50.981
logos are in ninety-four percent of ads and return one point four times.

00:01:51.031 --> 00:01:54.982
Sonic cues are in six percent and return eight point five.

00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:57.893
Three, strategy in action.

00:01:57.943 --> 00:02:02.327
Segmentation, targeting and positioning are business decisions with trade-offs.

00:02:02.377 --> 00:02:06.049
Get them right for a positive return on marketing investment.

00:02:06.733 --> 00:02:08.782
Four, brands shape culture.

00:02:08.832 --> 00:02:13.275
Harley-Davidson, Nike's Kaepernick ad and the RTA speeding campaign work.

00:02:13.325 --> 00:02:18.147
Pepsi's Kendall Jenner ad failed, borrowing a movement it had no standing in.

00:02:18.833 --> 00:02:23.866
Five, volatility moats: equity, reputation and crisis response.

00:02:23.916 --> 00:02:25.564
Tesla broke all three.

00:02:25.614 --> 00:02:28.759
Volkswagen survived Dieselgate on equity alone.

00:02:28.809 --> 00:02:32.191
KFC's FCK bucket rebuilt equity.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.659
Consumer equity grows most through referral from family and friends.

00:02:36.709 --> 00:02:37.720
Stop advertising and you fade from memory,

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:41.890
and getting back costs more than holding on.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:43.588
An unknown laptop brand can have the best specs and still no trust.

00:02:43.638 --> 00:02:50.894
Brands must be machine readable and human loveable, not AI slop.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.946
Marketing is a long game, often two to three years.

00:02:54.996 --> 00:02:57.458
Sampling breaks the uncertainty barrier.

00:02:57.508 --> 00:03:01.934
And in B2B, lean on the rational case and keep clear promises.

00:03:02.633 --> 00:03:06.062
So win before the point of choice, and audit your assets.

00:03:06.112 --> 00:03:07.583
Attention is cheap.

00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:09.328
Memory is expensive.

00:03:09.378 --> 00:03:11.097
Preference is priceless.

00:03:11.147 --> 00:03:13.519
That's week three.
